Subject: [PATCH] Diagnose the Supabase key, surface the install button,
 quieten Virginia
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

1. INVALID API KEY — this is configuration, not code, but the code was
   unhelpful about it. Supabase answers a wrong key with a bare 'Invalid
   API key', which tells the user nothing and the operator nothing.
   The anon key is a JWT carrying its own project ref, so we now decode
   it at startup and name the actual fault: truncated, whitespace, wrong
   project, wrong role, expired, or URL and key swapped. Logged to the
   console for the operator; the sign-in form now tells the visitor it is
   the site's problem and not theirs.
   Safe in the browser — the anon key is public by design and the check
   logs structural facts only, never the key.

2. PWA INSTALL — two likely reasons Chrome was not offering it.
   a) The SPA rewrite could match /sw.js and /manifest.webmanifest.
      Vercel checks the filesystem first so it probably did not, but if
      it ever had, registration fails silently and the prompt never
      appears. The rewrite now excludes them explicitly, and /sw.js gets
      no-cache plus Service-Worker-Allowed headers.
   b) Chrome no longer surfaces a visible prompt on most Android phones;
      the option sits in the three-dot menu where nobody looks. Added an
      Install button that captures beforeinstallprompt, so it appears
      only when Chrome has confirmed the site is installable and can
      never be a button that does nothing. Hidden once installed.

3. DEMO PRIORITY — Virginia was still a second large FeaturedCard,
   competing with John. She is now a quiet row: small portrait, Founding
   Memorial label, and the line 'the memorial MemoryGlen was built for'.
   Permanent and dignified, but no longer competing with the demo.
   John is now the only FeaturedCard on the directory.

4 tests. 65 pass in total.
